          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a neurological disorder characterized primarily by recurrent headaches. It is different from an ordinary headache in several significant ways. \n\nMigraine headaches are often one-sided, pulsating, and moderate to severe in intensity. They can worsen with routine physical activity and may be accompanied by symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, and sensitivity to light and sound. An untreated migraine attack can last from several hours to up to three days. In contrast, ordinary headaches, such as tension-type headaches, are generally less severe, often felt on both sides of the head, and do not typically include the additional symptoms associated with migraines.\n\nWhile migraines can be episodic or chronic (with chronic migraine defined as having headache on fifteen or more days per month, with at least eight showing migraine features), ordinary headaches do not have this same level of severity or frequency. This distinction is described in the educational summary on migraines from Consilium Health.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
